PRESTON MARKET UPDATE — we expect the proposed development plan to be released later this week.

We followed up last week’s questions at a briefing today.

It turns out that the draft plans align with the ‘Heart of Preston’ proposal submitted to the Standing Advisory Committee by council officers, NOT the version approved by councillors in 2021. That means that the draft plan:

— seeks to migrate the entire site to all-electric over time, with no new gas connections, but does not immediately ban all gas;

— retains all of the market north of Earle Street, but not the south shed;

— allows buildings up to 16 storeys, not 12, albeit with a smaller number of total apartments (as per the video);

— provides 10% affordable housing, not 20%, with the delivery model to be determined closer to the planning permit stage;

— provides 8% of the site as ground-level public open space, with additional elevated green space; and

— includes a potential new kindergarten or childcare centre, but not an MCH centre.

The development plan will be made available to the public through the Engage Victoria website so that everyone has the opportunity to review it and provide feedback directly to the Minister for Planning.

For those interested, the key design teams on the project are Six Degrees Architects, OCULUS, Macroplan, Mosquito Architecture and Sean Godsell Architects.

KEON PARK UPDATE — below are the issues people have raised regarding the Johnson Street social housing development:

— the potential parking implications, including the likelihood of adding to parking pressures around Johnson Street;
— the related issues of council parking enforcement and the difficulty of accessing the northern and southern station carparks from Johnson Street;
— the potential impact on pedestrian safety and traffic congestion, especially at the intersection of Johnson Street and Keon Parade;
— the apartment mix, particularly the proportion of one-bedroom apartments;
— the lack of private apartments or a mixed-tenure approach;
— the liveability of some apartment layouts;
— the adequacy of facilities and spaces for children;
— the adequacy of the communal space;
— the potential community safety and security issues;
— the support services that will be provided and their alignment with resident needs;
— the level of canopy cover and deep soil;
— the resilience to climate change;
— the energy efficiency;
— the need for an all-electric design;
— the need for a local greengrocer or fresh food retailer in Keon Park, and the potential for the ground-floor tenancy to serve that purpose;
— the general scale of the project;
— the design;
— the overlooking and overshadowing of nearby properties;
— the outsourcing of subsidised housing to non-government entities; and
— the importance of developer contributions.

Our understanding of the decision-making process is that Homes Victoria and @[100064139285623:2048:Hallmarc Group] will consider the feedback and submit their proposal to the Department of Transport and Planning. The community housing arrangements will be finalised with NAHV, Homes Victoria and Housing Australia. While the project is mainly federally funded, the Victorian Minister for Public Transport, the Treasurer and the Minister for Planning will need to provide approvals. 

We will raise the feedback points with all relevant ministers.

Any development at Preston Market would require two things: approved planning permits and an approved ‘development plan’ — in effect, a blueprint for how the whole site would work.

The development plan would include details of:

— the location and design of any new buildings;
— the protection of the existing market buildings;
— the location of new open space, landscaping and trees;
— the staging of any works; and
— support for traders during any construction.

We have asked at what point such a development plan would be made public.
